    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is Notion better than Evernote?</h3>
    <p>Notion is better for users who want an all-in-one workspace with notes, databases, wikis, and project management. It offers more flexibility and customization than Evernote. Evernote is better for users who want a focused note-taking app with strong OCR, document scanning, and offline access. For building a second brain or team wiki, Notion wins. For capturing and organizing reference material like receipts and PDFs, Evernote is stronger.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, Notion or Evernote?</h3>
    <p>Notion is cheaper. Notion Plus costs $10 per month, while Evernote Personal costs $14.99 per month. Notion also offers a more generous free plan with unlimited pages and blocks for individual users. Evernote's free plan limits users to 50 notes per month and 1 device. For budget-conscious users, Notion offers more value at a lower price.</p>

    <h3>Can I migrate from Evernote to Notion?</h3>
    <p>Yes, you can migrate from Evernote to Notion. Notion has a built-in Evernote importer that pulls notes, notebooks, and tags. The import preserves text formatting and images but does not perfectly map Evernote's notebook structure to Notion's database structure. Most users complete a migration in a few hours. For large Evernote libraries with thousands of notes, some manual reorganization is required after import.</p>

    <h3>Which has better search, Notion or Evernote?</h3>
    <p>Evernote has better search, especially for scanned documents and images. Evernote's OCR can find text inside images, PDFs, and scanned receipts, which Notion cannot do. Evernote also supports keyword search with advanced filters like tags, notebooks, and dates. Notion's search is functional but less powerful for finding text inside attachments. If you capture a lot of visual reference material, Evernote's search is significantly more capable.</p>

    <h3>Does Notion or Evernote work better offline?</h3>
    <p>Evernote works better offline. The desktop and mobile apps cache notes locally, so you can read and edit without an internet connection. Changes sync when you reconnect. Notion requires an internet connection for most operations. While Notion has added some offline support, it is limited and unreliable. If you frequently work without internet, such as on flights or in remote areas, Evernote is the better choice.</p>

    <h3>Can I use Notion and Evernote together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, some users use both apps together. A common setup is Evernote for capturing reference material like receipts, business cards, and scanned documents, and Notion for active projects, notes, and team wikis. This leverages Evernote's superior OCR and document scanning while using Notion for structured knowledge management. However, maintaining two systems adds overhead, so most users eventually pick one as their primary tool.</p>

